WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:06.950
Film Courage: The difference between editing,
revising and just plain skimming your work

00:00:06.950 --> 00:00:11.670
or a page you’ve done for the day, either
skimming it (and) praising it or skimming

00:00:11.670 --> 00:00:14.870
it (and) unfortunately trashing it (I mean
being critical of it)?

00:00:14.870 --> 00:00:19.050
So what are the differences between the three:
editing, revising and skimming?

00:00:19.050 --> 00:00:20.950
Or are they all kind of the same?

00:00:20.950 --> 00:00:25.380
Barrington Smith Seetachitt: Skimming is what
I do when I don’t know what I am going to

00:00:25.380 --> 00:00:26.380
do.

00:00:26.380 --> 00:00:30.550
Then you’re kind of…well okay.

00:00:30.550 --> 00:00:37.550
So editing, revising, skimming…interesting.

00:00:37.550 --> 00:00:39.350
I don’t know how often I ever skim.

00:00:39.350 --> 00:00:48.540
A skim would be like a copy edit or I literally
kind of…or I’m just tired and I’m just

00:00:48.540 --> 00:00:56.420
going to read this through and maybe…I can’t
remember was it Eric…Eric Roth, he’s a

00:00:56.420 --> 00:00:57.420
screenwriter?

00:00:57.420 --> 00:01:00.540
Is he the one who did (THE CURIOUS CASE OF)
BENJAMIN BUTTON?

00:01:00.540 --> 00:01:06.100
What he talks about when he writes, he always
starts at the beginning and reads through

00:01:06.100 --> 00:01:13.749
up to where he’s going to start writing
and then writes from there.

00:01:13.749 --> 00:01:19.340
And of course, in the reading through there
is little things you want to change so I’ll

00:01:19.340 --> 00:01:23.430
do that.

00:01:23.430 --> 00:01:28.880
And then there’s editing, like copy editing
and I make tons of typos so I definitely do

00:01:28.880 --> 00:01:31.329
that as well.

00:01:31.329 --> 00:01:37.679
Then I give it to other people to edit and
then I edit again and it’s still a mess.

00:01:37.679 --> 00:01:47.459
But revision, I try to approach it as a very
specific process whereby I go into it with

00:01:47.459 --> 00:01:48.670
a plan.

00:01:48.670 --> 00:01:54.359
I’m like okay, this is a scene and it’s
weak and the scene has these building blocks

00:01:54.359 --> 00:02:02.039
and there is a certain beginning, middle and
end and it needs some kind of adjustment and

00:02:02.039 --> 00:02:07.759
so I try to look within that scene at kind
of the polarity.

00:02:07.759 --> 00:02:12.620
Like there is usually a little tiny character
arc within the scene, right?

00:02:12.620 --> 00:02:21.930
Somebody opens the door for their date, hopeful,
and then they have a little conversation and

00:02:21.930 --> 00:02:25.400
by the time they get to the car, they’re
feeling like “This is going to be great!”

00:02:25.400 --> 00:02:30.060
or by the time they get to the car they’re
like “This is already a disaster” and

00:02:30.060 --> 00:02:31.420
those are two different things.

00:02:31.420 --> 00:02:36.040
And so a lot of times I think the revision
process is where you’ve kind of looked at

00:02:36.040 --> 00:02:41.969
the scene in terms of tracking what the character
is going to go through throughout the entire

00:02:41.969 --> 00:02:43.239
script.

00:02:43.239 --> 00:02:45.900
Where does this scene fit into it?

00:02:45.900 --> 00:02:47.549
How is it setting up for other things?

00:02:47.549 --> 00:02:48.549
What does it need to do?

00:02:48.549 --> 00:02:54.409
So I look at it in terms of function and in
terms of those building blocks and so when

00:02:54.409 --> 00:02:59.640
I revise I have a very “Now it does this
and now I either want it to do this better

00:02:59.640 --> 00:03:03.540
or I want it to do something that’s different.”

00:03:03.540 --> 00:03:05.879
That’s my ideal revision.

00:03:05.879 --> 00:03:08.129
You don’t always get that luxury.

00:03:08.129 --> 00:03:09.200
Sometimes you’re just like “This sucks!

00:03:09.200 --> 00:03:12.359
And somehow I’ve got to make it seem better.”

00:03:12.359 --> 00:03:20.180
And then you’re just fiddling but revision
in its ideal form for me is that.

00:03:20.180 --> 00:03:25.940
Janice Rhoshalle Littlejohn: Editing, skimming
and revision?

00:03:25.940 --> 00:03:31.490
I think to a certain extent I agree with all
of that.

00:03:31.490 --> 00:03:47.510
For me, when I’m writing something I do
tend to just write out, just completely and

00:03:47.510 --> 00:03:55.999
then go back and read what I’ve written
before I go to bed or before I give it up

00:03:55.999 --> 00:04:04.299
and see if I can go a little and do a little
of the tweaking and, even to a certain extent,

00:04:04.299 --> 00:04:11.620
see how much further I can go until I hit
a wall and then the next morning I go back

00:04:11.620 --> 00:04:14.799
and re-read.

00:04:14.799 --> 00:04:22.960
I do a lot of self-editing, whether it be
for small words or how can I say this better?

00:04:22.960 --> 00:04:26.560
Or how can it sound more real?

00:04:26.560 --> 00:04:31.120
How can it sound more authentic?

00:04:31.120 --> 00:04:38.680
Because I think as a journalist I write differently
than I speak and so one of the challenges

00:04:38.680 --> 00:04:47.860
for me with dialogue has been listening to
how people speak and to the cadence to which

00:04:47.860 --> 00:04:54.240
people speak and that’s why going to café’s
was important to me because getting the rhythm

00:04:54.240 --> 00:04:58.660
of a particular speech pattern was important
to me.

00:04:58.660 --> 00:05:04.310
I would listen to my friends in a different…actually…screenwriting
made me a better listener.

00:05:04.310 --> 00:05:09.150
Because I would listen to what people were
saying and how they were saying it and what

00:05:09.150 --> 00:05:11.970
inflections they gave it.

00:05:11.970 --> 00:05:18.319
So when I edit am I keeping that rhythm?

00:05:18.319 --> 00:05:20.730
Is that cadence still flowing?

00:05:20.730 --> 00:05:24.270
So I’m looking and editing for specific
things.

00:05:24.270 --> 00:05:29.110
And then with the revision (like what Barrington
was saying), you know, I’m looking at the

00:05:29.110 --> 00:05:35.380
overall and what can make the overall better.

00:05:35.380 --> 00:05:38.569
I guess the editing and skimming kind of go
hand-in-hand for me.

00:05:38.569 --> 00:05:48.440
It’s like I’m doing that all at one time
looking for different things but I actually

00:05:48.440 --> 00:05:54.470
like the process of editing and I’m working
as an editor now for Los Angeles Review of

00:05:54.470 --> 00:06:02.259
Books and working on other people’s work
and looking at how to make their work better

00:06:02.259 --> 00:06:08.669
because that was always one of the things
that I loved about my editors is that they

00:06:08.669 --> 00:06:14.111
kept the voice that I was looking for and
my voice in the story.

00:06:14.111 --> 00:06:16.199
But they just made it that much better.

00:06:16.199 --> 00:06:18.840
And so that’s what editing for me is.

00:06:18.840 --> 00:06:22.560
It’s a way to make things better.

00:06:22.560 --> 00:06:28.250
Not always did editors make it better but
those that did, that was where I found you

00:06:28.250 --> 00:06:31.830
know, a strength.

00:06:31.830 --> 00:06:36.990
And keeping that voice and making sure the
best of that voice came through out.

00:06:36.990 --> 00:06:45.430
So what I hope when I’m editing is just
honing in on those things that really make

00:06:45.430 --> 00:06:52.790
that voice sound authentic and I think to
our credit one of the things that people said

00:06:52.790 --> 00:06:58.159
was that each of the characters had their
own distinct voice and so that was really

00:06:58.159 --> 00:07:05.479
gratifying and so now it’s just the revising
and making sure all that great voice is in

00:07:05.479 --> 00:07:14.990
context with a great story and a strong sensibility
and good conflict and deep, meaningful you

00:07:14.990 --> 00:07:19.050
know…great…deep…meaningful Barrington
Smith Seetachitt: Story!

00:07:19.050 --> 00:07:23.120
[Laughs]
Janice Rhoshalle Littlejohn: Story…yes…that

00:07:23.120 --> 00:07:28.009
was a word I couldn’t seem to get out [laughs].

00:07:28.009 --> 00:07:33.060
Barrington Smith Seetachitt: And something
you said in there also made me think of kind

00:07:33.060 --> 00:07:39.379
of you know, I think my mind tends to jump
to this kind of prose editing but I think

00:07:39.379 --> 00:07:45.729
hearing her talk about voice, one another
kind of editing that you’ll do is you’ll

00:07:45.729 --> 00:07:46.940
take a pass.

00:07:46.940 --> 00:07:54.289
So you’ll do a dialogue pass, especially
if…we don’t get to do as many passes as

00:07:54.289 --> 00:08:01.080
we like because it seems like we’re always
writing to a deadline but given the amount

00:08:01.080 --> 00:08:07.960
of time, like you’ll do a dialogue pass,
and then you’ll do kind of a pass for geography

00:08:07.960 --> 00:08:10.180
and a pass for certain characters.

00:08:10.180 --> 00:08:17.199
Like you’ll do a pass where you’re just
kind of tracking where one character is emotionally.

00:08:17.199 --> 00:08:23.569
You know, do they have a logical progression
of emotion between if you know where the beginning

00:08:23.569 --> 00:08:28.410
is and you know where the end is or is there
some place in the middle where they are in

00:08:28.410 --> 00:08:32.900
a scene but you’re getting kind of lost
and you can just put in a reaction that shows

00:08:32.900 --> 00:08:37.810
that something is changing in them if they
are reacting in a certain way to what is going

00:08:37.810 --> 00:08:49.250
on, so that it’s something that you see
on the screen more, right?

00:08:49.250 --> 00:08:50.770
So you do your main character pass.

00:08:50.770 --> 00:08:54.030
Sometimes you’ll do a pass for secondary
characters.

00:08:54.030 --> 00:08:57.010
So you’ll do those kind of different passes
and I guess that would also fall under editorial.

00:08:57.010 --> 00:08:58.060
Question for the Viewers: What thoughts on
editing a screenplay can you add?

